DESCRIPTION >
    Per-balance hourly rollup of the `deductions` array on `events`, keyed
    (org_id, env, customer_id, balance_feature_id, balance_id, event_name, entity_id, hour).

    Grain: one row per (customer, balance, tracked feature, entity, hour). Answers "for one customer
    in one hour, how much came out of each balance, split by which tracked feature caused it and
    which entity did it".

    Exists so the read path never decodes JSON. `events.deductions` is stored double-encoded —
    {"list": ["<json string>", ...]}, an array of JSON *strings* — so reading one field costs an
    ARRAY JOIN plus two nested JSONExtract calls per field per row. That work happens once here at
    insert instead of per request.

    NO PROPERTIES. Deliberate: property values like session_id are unbounded, and a property fan-out
    is what caused the write amplification on events_property_mv. Every dimension in this rollup is
    bounded per customer (balances, tracked features, entities), so there is no key a high-cardinality
    value can reach. group_by on properties in deducted mode reads `events` directly instead — that
    path is bounded because customer_id is mandatory and leads the events sorting key.

    Row growth is ~1:1 with events before the hourly GROUP BY: 99.9% of events carry exactly one
    deduction, max observed is 3. Events with an empty deduction set produce no rows at all, since
    ARRAY JOIN over an empty array drops them.

    NOT STORED HERE, resolved server-side at response assembly:
      - feature_type (metered | credit_system)  -> from ctx.features by balance_feature_id
      - credit_cost                             -> from the credit system's config.schema; reflects
                                                   the CURRENT schema, not the rate at deduction time
      - balances[].entity_id (the balance owner) -> from the customer's entitlements by balance_id.
                                                   `entity_id` in this rollup is the EVENT's entity,
                                                   which is the one group_by uses.

    plan_id and reset_interval are functionally dependent on balance_id, so including them in the
    GROUP BY adds no rows. Both are '' when the deduction carried null — a balance created outside a
    plan (handleCreateBalance) has no plan.

    `deducted` may be negative: a finalize-lock unwind returns credit.

    BACKFILL skip: deploy empty + forward-materialization trigger only. The deploy-time
    repopulate runs the ARRAY JOIN + JSON decode over ALL of `events` in one query and
    blows the 10s deployment cap at prod scale. History is filled in bounded chunks on
    on-demand compute via the events_deductions_hourly_mv_backfill copy pipe.

SCHEMA >
    `org_id` String,
    `env` String,
    `customer_id` String,
    `entity_id` String DEFAULT '',
    `event_name` String,
    `balance_feature_id` String,
    `balance_id` String,
    `plan_id` String DEFAULT '',
    `reset_interval` String DEFAULT '',
    `hour` DateTime,
    `deducted` Float64,
    `deduction_count` UInt64

ENGINE "MergeTree"
ENGINE_PARTITION_KEY "toYYYYMM(hour)"
ENGINE_SORTING_KEY "org_id, env, customer_id, balance_feature_id, balance_id, event_name, entity_id, hour"

BACKFILL skip
